1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:11,050
If only one transaction in a hundred is fraud, a model that always says not fraud is ninety-nine percent accurate, and completely useless.

2
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:20,250
So we look closer, with the confusion matrix: true positives, false positives, false negatives and true negatives.

3
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:29,825
A fraud model flags fifty transactions. Forty are really fraud. There were eighty frauds in total.

4
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:40,920
Precision: forty out of fifty, eighty percent. Recall: forty out of eighty, fifty percent. It is accurate when it rings, but misses half.

5
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:46,570
F one combines both into one number, when you need a balance.

6
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:57,630
Precision asks: when the alarm rings, how often is it right? Recall asks: of all the real cases, how many did we catch?

7
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:06,780
A cancer screen wants high recall. A spam filter wants high precision. Choose by the cost of each mistake.

